$DIR/main.p:39:2: cannot store readAll(3).Slice() in s, which outlives the temporary
    readAll(3).Slice() points into the temporary Buf that readAll(3) returns.
    The Buf that readAll(3) returns (at main.p:39) is a temporary: nothing keeps
    it, so it is the receiver of the method only until the end of the statement.
    A view of it may be passed to calls in that statement but may not outlive
    it. At the end of the statement, the temporary is dropped, but s lives
    longer, so it would be left pointing at a value that has been dropped.
    In Go a pointer method needs an addressable operand, so the result of a call
    has no pointer methods. Plutonium lets a temporary owner be the receiver for
    its statement, so that a call such as readAll(f).Slice() can be passed
    straight on, and drops the temporary when the statement ends, since nothing
    owns it.
    Keep the owner in a variable, so that it lives until the end of its scope,
    and call the method on the variable:
        v := readAll(3)
        ... v.Slice() ...
    (see "Calls" in the Plutonium spec, section 11.14)
    (run "pluto explain E3009" for a longer explanation)

